Hi Alex — adding some concrete guidance from the engineering side so your infra team can review ahead of the technical working session.

1) SDK & auth (server-side recommended):
- Use the official Redwood SDK (node/python) with long-lived server API keys stored in your KMS.
- For private deployments, we support mTLS or customer-managed KMS for signing requests.

2) Request headers & idempotency:
- Include X-Request-Id: <uuid> for traceability.
- For mutating endpoints (if any), include Idempotency-Key: <client-generated-id> so retries are safe; Redwood will dedupe within a 24h window by default.

3) Rate limiting & quotas:
- Per-API-key rate limit: token-based QPS limit configured per plan (we can provision Dedicated capacity for predictable throughput).
- We expose headers on responses: X-RateLimit-Limit, X-RateLimit-Remaining, X-RateLimit-Reset.

4) 429 handling & retries:
- Respect Retry-After when present (seconds).
- Best-effort strategy: jittered exponential backoff: base=200ms, multiplier=2, max_backoff=10s, max_attempts=5.
- For idempotent requests use a linear retry with small jitter for short-lived failures; for non-idempotent require Idempotency-Key.

5) Retries & partial results:
- For streaming/long-running responses we return a resume token in the body if available; clients should use resume token to fetch remaining output rather than blind retries.

6) Embeddings & rerankers:
- Bulk embedding requests are rate-limited separately (per-embedding QPS). We recommend batching up to 256 texts per request if your average length is small.
- Reranker: use a single-shot rerank endpoint for top-K candidates (send candidate IDs + embeddings) to avoid repeated calls.

Code snippet (pseudo):
- Node (retry wrapper):
  async function callWithRetry(req) {
    for (let i=0; i<5; i++) {
      try {
        req.headers['X-Request-Id'] = uuid();
        if (isMutating(req)) req.headers['Idempotency-Key'] = generateId();
        return await redwood.client.call(req);
      } catch (err) {
        if (!isRetryable(err) || i === 4) throw err;
        const backoff = Math.min(100 * Math.pow(2, i) + randomJitter(), 10000);
        await sleep(backoff);
      }
    }
  }

I’ll be on the follow-up; we can tune the backoff params after we see your QPS/burst numbers.

